WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.612
Governance is the system that makes secure cloud behavior repeatable. It is not just a policy document and it is not just a security team's dashboard.

2
00:00:10.732 --> 00:00:22.569
Good governance helps teams structure accounts, subscriptions, projects, resources, identities, budgets, ownership, and exceptions so that secure choices are easier to make and risky changes are easier to find.

3
00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:29.220
Clear structure matters. Accounts, subscriptions, projects, folders, and resource groups should reflect how the organization operates.

4
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.646
Production, development, shared services, security tooling, and experimental work may need different boundaries.

5
00:00:34.766 --> 00:00:41.704
Naming and tagging help people find assets, understand purpose, assign owners, track data sensitivity, and route alerts.

6
00:00:41.824 --> 00:00:49.580
A resource with no owner is hard to review. A resource with no environment label is hard to prioritize.

7
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:56.230
A resource with no data classification may be treated too casually. Baselines define expected secure configuration.

8
00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:08.595
A baseline might require logging, private storage by default, encryption at rest, multi-factor authentication for privileged users, restricted administrator roles, backup policies, tagging, approved regions, or limited public exposure.

9
00:01:08.715 --> 00:01:20.552
The important point is not that every workload is identical. The point is that teams know the secure starting point and document exceptions when they need a different design.

10
00:01:20.672 --> 00:01:26.386
Infrastructure as code can support governance because it makes configuration reviewable, repeatable, and versioned.

11
00:01:26.506 --> 00:01:33.037
A change to a network rule, role policy, or storage setting can be reviewed before deployment.

12
00:01:33.157 --> 00:01:42.136
But infrastructure as code is not automatically secure. Templates must be maintained, modules must be reviewed, and pipelines must have appropriate permissions.

13
00:01:42.256 --> 00:01:51.236
Otherwise automation can create consistent mistakes at scale. Drift detection helps teams find when actual configuration no longer matches the intended baseline.

14
00:01:51.356 --> 00:01:57.887
Drift can come from emergency changes, manual console edits, old experiments, service updates, or incomplete cleanup.

15
00:01:58.007 --> 00:02:09.843
The response should be practical: identify the owner, understand the reason, fix the configuration or approve an exception, and update the baseline if the old rule no longer fits.

16
00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:16.494
Continuous improvement is the right mindset. Cloud security is not finished after the first hardening pass.

17
00:02:16.614 --> 00:02:27.634
Services change, teams change, applications change, and risk changes. Regular review, clear ownership, safe exception handling, posture reporting, and remediation tracking turn governance into daily operating discipline.

18
00:02:27.754 --> 00:02:39.183
The goal is a cloud environment where teams can move quickly while still knowing what exists, who owns it, which controls apply, and how risky changes are corrected.
